  Status: author draft; neither Lean correspondence nor mathematical review
  has been performed. Source revision inspected:
  <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>.

  <section|Scope and local notation><label|scope-and-local-notation>

  The LaTeX section <verbatim|manuscript/existence/limits.tex> drafts the
  mesh-then-volume extraction and the transfer of exact Euclidean identities
  and positivity under an ordered limit. It uses internal family notation
  <verbatim|C> as <verbatim|\\mathcal C>, table <verbatim|S> as the Euclidean
  correlation table, and <verbatim|f.mesh>, <verbatim|f.per> as the two
  strictly increasing index sequences. No public Wilson family
  <verbatim|\\mathcal W> or public volume request <verbatim|\\varrho> is
  identified with these internal data here.

  <section|Correspondence inspected><label|correspondence-inspected>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Manuscript
  content>>|<cell|<strong|Source declaration>>|<cell|<strong|Inspected
  meaning>>>|<row|<cell|Iterated mesh-then-period limit; refinement
  stability>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.limits>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.retained>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Orders.lean>>|<cell|Eventual eligibility along
  <verbatim|f.per>; for every eligible fixed period the mesh row converges
  along <verbatim|f.mesh>; the row limits converge along <verbatim|f.per>.
  <verbatim|retained> transports the supplied limits along a further pair of
  strict subsequences. It proves no compactness.>>|<row|<cell|Mesh uniform
  approximation and volume compact-uniform
  convergence>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.Meshes>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.VolumePoints>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.PointLimits>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.evaluated>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Limits.lean>>|<cell|<verbatim|Meshes.onK> is a
  compact-patch lattice-point approximation with a mesh-tail threshold.
  <verbatim|VolumePoints> is compact-uniform convergence of the intermediate
  tables along the volume sequence. <verbatim|PointLimits> packages both.
  <verbatim|evaluated> passes the mesh approximation through a convergent
  sequence of grid points.>>|<row|<cell|Explicit finite-cutoff inputs to
  equicontinuity>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.TransferLip>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Lipschitz.lean>>|<cell|<verbatim|grid>: for
  each fixed period and compact subset of its cyclic patch, an eventual
  lattice modulus. <verbatim|expanding>: for compact generic sets, a modulus
  in the <verbatim|f.late> ordered filter; its mesh threshold may depend on
  the outer period index.>>|<row|<cell|Explicit finite-cutoff size
  inputs>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Bound>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Bound.further>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean>>|<cell|<verbatim|cyclic>: an
  eventual mesh bound at each fixed period and compact <verbatim|NoHit> set.
  <verbatim|volume>: a bound eventually in the ordered mesh/period filter on
  compact subsets of <verbatim|conf>.>>|<row|<cell|Common compactness
  extraction>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.localAscoli>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Tame.lean>; <verbatim|YM.Extraction.setMeshes>
  and <verbatim|YM.Extraction.first_surface>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Lipschitz.lean>>|<cell|<verbatim|localAscoli>
  uses a countable index, a countable dense subset per locally compact
  second-countable metric space, compact scalar disks, and oscillation to
  produce one subsequence and compact-uniform continuous limits.
  <verbatim|setMeshes> first extracts intermediate mesh limits for all
  countable signatures. <verbatim|first_surface> then selects one common
  period subsequence and returns <verbatim|RawLimits>: generic continuity,
  <verbatim|Meshes>, <verbatim|VolumePoints>, and
  <verbatim|Ordered>.>>|<row|<cell|Smearing limit from extracted
  fields>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.intraperiod>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.ordinary>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.poses>, <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean>>|<cell|Compact
  cutoffs and <verbatim|Bound> give an integrable dominating function;
  pointwise convergence of cell densities gives the fixed-period integral
  limit. Outer volume dominated convergence uses the ordered volume bounds.
  <verbatim|poses> turns <verbatim|PointLimits> and <verbatim|Bound> into
  <verbatim|Ordered>.>>|<row|<cell|Exact lattice identities used in Euclidean
  transfer>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Family.oriented>,
  <verbatim|YM.Family.anchored>, <verbatim|YM.Family.real_eval>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/LatticeFamily.lean>;
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.limits_basic>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Inherited.lean>>|<cell|Under
  <verbatim|C.Makes> and eventual token depth, these give finite-level signed
  symmetry, lattice translation anchoring, and conjugation identities. The
  generic point limits and continuity are what extend them; finite lattice
  translations alone do not give arbitrary continuum
  translations.>>|<row|<cell|Exact bound/modulus producer
  requirements>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.CenteredBook>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.CenterPort.book>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Center.lean>;
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Difference.completed>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Equicontinuity.lean>;
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Held.size_done>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Held.lean>>|<cell|<verbatim|CenteredBook.each>
  is quantified token, fixed period, eventually depth, an explicit
  nonnegative budget, then eventually mesh; <verbatim|expanding> is token,
  eventually depth, budget, then ordered eventuality in mesh and period.
  <verbatim|CenterPort.book> additionally uses its <verbatim|cells>
  hypothesis. Under <verbatim|C.Makes>, <verbatim|Difference.completed>
  derives <verbatim|TransferLip>; <verbatim|Held.size_done> derives
  <verbatim|Bound>. These are proof obligations about the actual finite
  trajectory, not consequences of <verbatim|Order.limits>.>>|<row|<cell|Euclidean
  passage>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.limits_basic>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.realPositive>,
  <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Order.assembles>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Inherited.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction/Harvest.lean>>|<cell|With <verbatim|C.Makes>,
  <verbatim|S.Smooth>, <verbatim|PointLimits>, and <verbatim|Ordered>, source
  proves symmetry/basic identities, homogeneity, real-spin identities, and
  reflection-positive smeared form; <verbatim|assembles> additionally needs
  <verbatim|S.Capped> and packages a <verbatim|Hierarchy>. Smoothness through
  ties is an explicit prerequisite here.>>|<row|<cell|Joint endpoint, which
  is not just compactness>|<cell|<verbatim|YM.RG17.supplies>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/RG17/Joint.lean>; <verbatim|YM.Spec.Final>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Spec/Closed.lean>; <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.Final.from_spec>,
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/FullVolume/Finale/Program.lean>>|<cell|<verbatim|supplies>
  returns a large bundle and obtains <verbatim|F : Spec.Final> through
  <verbatim|Running.extraction>, <verbatim|extractible17>, phase
  reconstruction and a final assembly. <verbatim|Spec.Final> already contains
  a <verbatim|Seed> with <verbatim|Ordered>, <verbatim|PointLimits>,
  hierarchy, cell hypotheses and corrected estimates, plus algebra, selected
  phase and relativistic reconstruction. <verbatim|from_spec> merely projects
  an ordered result from that package using separate UV-position data and
  uniqueness. It is not an Ascoli or Prokhorov theorem.>>>>>

  <section|Exact estimate boundary and remaining
  obligations><label|exact-estimate-boundary-and-remaining-obligations>

  The first local extraction theorem, <verbatim|YM.Extraction.first_surface>,
  does not establish its own estimates. It gives limits only along a mesh
  subsequence; the full-mesh upgrade at the public endpoint is a separate
  input, not an output of this compactness argument. Its exact inputs are
  <verbatim|TransferLip C f> and <verbatim|Bound C f>. The inspected
  producers reduce those to <verbatim|CenteredBook C f> and
  <verbatim|C.Makes>. Thus the first finite-cutoff estimate to independently
  establish for an application is either the displayed
  <verbatim|CenteredBook> budget (including the fixed-period and expanding
  quantifiers) together with <verbatim|C.Makes>, or a direct alternative
  yielding both clauses of <verbatim|TransferLip> and both clauses of
  <verbatim|Bound>. In <verbatim|CenterPort.book>, the <verbatim|cells>
  component is also required; no weaker \Peventually in one diagonal\Q
  estimate suffices. The manuscript keeps this boundary explicit and does not
  claim that the abstract compactness step proves these inputs.

  <verbatim|first_surface> only returns generic-continuous
  <verbatim|RawLimits>. It does not prove smoothness at coincident
  configurations. The hierarchy passage <verbatim|Harvest.assembles> requires
  <verbatim|S.Smooth> and <verbatim|S.Capped>, so these remain separate
  dependencies. A second caveat is quantifier scope: the source selects a
  common subsequence over the countable signature index, then uses compact
  equicontinuity to extend from dense sets. This establishes limits for every
  signature in that countable index; it is not a raw diagonal choice over
  uncountably many arbitrary test functions.

  No literature note in <verbatim|blueprint/background/README.md> supplies
  this exact Wilson-lattice estimate package or the source-specific limit
  passage. The compactness and positivity arguments in the draft are given
  directly; the standard countable-product compactness and
  dominated-convergence facts should be checked by reviewers against the
  stated spaces and hypotheses. No external source is cited as replacing the
  model-specific estimate obligations.

